\subsection{Failure Mode Analysis}
\label{sec:failure_modes}

Three failure modes reveal where and why the pipeline breaks down, each isolating a distinct distribution shift between CRM training and deployment (Table~\ref{tab:failure_modes}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Three failure modes of the correction pipeline, each corresponding to a distribution shift that causes CRM to degrade or fail.}
\label{tab:failure_modes}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Failure mode & Mechanism & Success (\%) & Ref. \\
\midrule
$K{=}1$ correction & Input shift & 10.3 & \S\ref{sec:main_results} \\
LTO (held-out tasks) & Task shift & 2.0 & This section \\
perdof uncapped & Feature shift & 0.0 & \S\ref{sec:magnitude_results} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Input distribution shift ($K{=}1$).}
At $K{=}1$, the CRM receives an arbitrary single sample rather than the argmin-centered input it was trained on.
This input distribution mismatch causes correction to fall below the uncorrected baseline (10.3\% vs.\ 11.6\%), confirming that multi-sampling is an enabling condition, not merely an optional enhancement.

\paragraph{Task distribution shift (leave-task-out).}
A leave-task-out (LTO) evaluation trains the CRM on 15 tasks and evaluates on 5 held-out tasks.
Success drops to 2\% versus 14\% for VLA-only on the held-out set: the CRM actively degrades performance on unseen tasks.
Learned corrections are task-specific; the module memorizes correction patterns tied to the calibration data distribution.
The CRM must be calibrated on data representative of the target task distribution, analogous to how conformal prediction requires exchangeable calibration data~\cite{angelopoulos2023gentle}.
All results in Table~\ref{tab:main} are in-distribution (the CRM training set covers all 20 LIBERO tasks).

\paragraph{Feature distribution shift (perdof collapse).}
Loosening $\varepsilon$ exposes the perdof variant to noisy correction directions from poorly estimated per-DOF covariances, causing progressive collapse from 14\% ($\varepsilon{=}0.03$) to 0\% (uncapped, 3 seeds).
The same architecture with observation-only conditioning remains stable across all tested bounds (Section~\ref{sec:magnitude_results}), isolating the failure to conformal feature quality rather than correction magnitude.

\paragraph{Unified interpretation.}
All three modes share a common mechanism: the CRM produces harmful corrections when its input departs from the training distribution.
Multi-sampling provides the correct input distribution (centering), task-matched calibration data provides the correct correction targets, and observation-only conditioning avoids amplifying estimation noise.
The pipeline's effectiveness is bounded by distribution alignment between CRM training and deployment across all three axes.

