All three modes share a common mechanism: the CRM produces harmful corrections when its input departs from the training distribution, whether through missing centering, unseen tasks, or noisy features.

\paragraph{Inference-time scaling with $K$.}
$K{=}5$ captures ${\sim}$82\% of the $K{=}10$ benefit at half the compute ($16.3$\% vs.\ $17.6$\%), making it the recommended configuration. At $K{=}1$, correction alone falls below baseline ($10.3$\% vs.\ $11.6$\%) because the single sample lacks argmin centering. Full analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:k_scaling}.

\paragraph{Applicability boundary.}
The pipeline requires stochastic action diversity. OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (autoregressive tokenization, 7B) produces 8/10 identical samples at temperature $T{=}0.5$, with per-DOF variance below $10^{-30}$, compared to SmolVLA's meaningfully diverse flow-matching samples. Multi-sample correction is specific to stochastic generative VLAs; tokenized VLAs lack the required diversity (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}, Figure~\ref{fig:diversity}).

\paragraph{Per-task analysis.}
Improvement concentrates on medium-difficulty tasks (e.g., chocolate\_pudding: 20\%$\to$60\%); tasks where the base policy fails completely remain uncorrectable (Appendix~\ref{sec:per_task}).

\paragraph{Computational overhead.}
The CRM adds $<$\,10\,ms per step ($<$\,0.5\% of total pipeline time), making correction overhead negligible.
The bottleneck is VLA sampling: at the recommended $K{=}5$, flow-matching passes take ${\sim}$0.9\,s (${\sim}$4.3$\times$ single inference; prefix encoding is shared), yielding ${\sim}$1.1\,Hz control. Scaling to $K{=}10$ increases latency to ${\sim}$1.8\,s (${\sim}$0.56\,Hz) for a marginal +1.3\,pp gain.
Batched forward passes that fully share prefix computation can reduce multi-sample latency toward single-inference time; this engineering optimization does not affect the analytical conclusions of this work. Full timing breakdown in Appendix~\ref{sec:latency}.

