\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented a sample-select-correct pipeline for frozen flow-matching VLAs with a lightweight CRM trained on task-specific VLA rollout data.
On in-distribution LIBERO-Object tasks, $K{=}5$ achieves $16.3 \pm 2.2$\% vs.\ $11.6 \pm 1.1$\% ($p = 0.016$); learned correction is the active mechanism, with multi-sampling as an enabling condition.
Three failure modes (Section~\ref{sec:failure_modes}) share a unifying pattern: the CRM degrades when its input departs from the training distribution. On LIBERO-Spatial, correction provides no benefit, bounding the scope to object-centric manipulation.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
All experiments use LIBERO in simulation with SmolVLA (450M); real-robot transfer and larger VLAs are untested. 16.3\% mean success is far from deployment-ready; A2C2~\cite{a2c2_2025} reports 67--72\% with full SmolVLA training on the same benchmark.

\paragraph{AI disclosure.}
AI tools (Claude, GitHub Copilot) assisted with code development and manuscript drafting. All results and claims were verified by the authors.
